   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 2.3. Increased capacity of Member States to engage in a forward-looking international labour standards policy</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Ministry of Labour and Employment (MoLE) submitted reports due in 2022 (C005, C011, C029, C081, C090, C105, C123, C138, C141, C144, C182, MLC) before 1 September 2022 and provided replies to the comments of the ILO supervisory bodies. MoLE submitted reports due for  2023 on C088, C100, C111, C122, and C142 in 2023 responding to the comments of the ILO supervisory bodies before 1 September 2023. ILO organized workshop in July 2022 to strengthen the capacity of the MoLE officials to prepare informative reports to the Committee of Experts on Application of Conventions and Recommendations (CEACR), responding to the comments of the supervisory bodies. 
In 2023, ILO held meetings with the MoLE officials to discuss the development of the reports to the CEACR. 
Three representatives from India participated in the ILO/ITC (International Training Centre), Turin, International Labour Standards Academy (Asia and the Pacific) in May 2022. 
In August 2022, ILO supported training for national trade unions in India on preparation of trade unions&#x2019; observations to the CEACR; consultation on the same was conducted in June 2023. Central trade unions submitted observations to the CEACR on C81 and C144 in 2022; on C001, C089, C144 in 2023.</narrative>
   </description>
